>
> To whom it may concern,
>
> I am writing to appeal the variance order listed above. While many city
> policies have been adopted to encourage development of residential zoning
> developments, it appears that very little was done to ensure that propert=
y
> regulations and research was properly integrated into the planning for th=
is
> project. We respectfully appeal against the approval of the proposed
> variance application, as it disregards proper procedure and would
> significantly impact the tenants' ability to enjoy the services they are
> entitled to.
>
> Firstly, it has come to our attention that the Applicant intends to remov=
e
> the community laundry and storage facilities, which are essential service=
s
> provided to the tenants. According to a statement provided by Celena Funk=
,
> Property Manager of 585 Corydon Ave, on May 5, 2023, the Applicant
> acknowledged that the withdrawal of such services requires an application
> to the Residential Tenancies Branch. It is concerning that the Applicant
> has failed to follow this required procedure, raising doubts about the
> legitimacy of their variance application. It is impossible that an
> application for withdrawal of services has occurred and been processed by
> May 11, 2023. As such, the variance cannot be completed until after revie=
w
> by the Residential Tenancies Branch.
>
> Furthermore, Section 169 of the City of Winnipeg Zoning By-law states tha=
t
> a variance application can only be approved if it does not create a
> substantial adverse effect on the amenities, use, safety, and convenience
> of the surrounding properties and area. It is important to note that the
> provision of common areas, services, facilities, and privileges, includin=
g
> laundry and storage, is an integral part of the rental agreement between
> the tenants and the landlord. Removing these services would directly impa=
ct
> the tenants' ability to fully enjoy the amenities and conveniences they p=
ay
> for and are entitled to under their tenancy agreement.
>
> Additionally, the cost of rent is not solely based on the rental unit
> itself but also includes the use of common areas, services, and facilitie=
s.
> If the laundry and storage facilities are removed, it is only fair that t=
he
> cost of rent be adjusted accordingly since these services would no longer
> be provided. This is not a minor matter, as the removal of these services
> would have a significant effect on both the property and the tenants. No
> alternative has been provided for this tenant.
>
> Considering these factors, we strongly believe that the proposed variance
> is not desirable and should be rejected. Removing essential services
> without following the proper procedures not only undermines the rights an=
d
> expectations of the tenants, but also raises concerns about the Applicant=
's
> compliance with relevant regulations and obligations.
>
> In light of the above, we kindly request that the Committee carefully
> considers the concerns raised in this letter during your evaluation of
> Thorwin Properties' variance application. We urge you to prioritize the
> protection of tenants' rights, the preservation of essential services, an=
d
> the adherence to proper procedures in land development applications.
>
> Thank you for your attention to this matter. We trust that the Committee
> will act in the best interest of the tenants and the community as a whole=
.
> Should you require any further information or clarification, please do no=
t
> hesitate to contact me at your earliest convenience.
>
> Sincerely,
> Evan Rosner
